course: dictionary
lesson: 7070
title: Patois: a language of its own
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A patois is a form of language spoken by ordinary people in one particular area. It is different from the standard language you read in books and hear on the news. A patois is not just broken English. It is a full way of speaking, with its own rules.
explain: Jamaican Patois, often spelled «Patwa», is the everyday language of Jamaica. Zara-Leigh's grandma rings from Jamaica and says, «Wah gwaan, mi baby?» The words around it help: she is on the phone to her grandchild, so wah gwaan must be asking what's going on.
explain: A patois can also mean the special talk of a group. Read the sentence around the word to find out which meaning the writer means. In a story, a character who speaks patois often shows where they come from.
fact: A patois is a form of a language spoken by ordinary people in a particular area.
fact: Jamaican Patois grew from English mixed with West African languages, and it has its own grammar.
fact: «Wah gwaan» means what's going on, and it is heard in UK slang too.
fact: «Mi soon come» means I'll be there soon, though maybe later.
fact: Patois is spoken by millions of people, so it is not «bad English».
exercise: In «Wah gwaan, mi baby?», what does wah gwaan mean? | I am very tired | *What's going on? | Let's go home | Where are you going?
exercise: The phrase «Mi soon come» means: | Come here at once | Go away quickly | *I'll be there soon, though maybe later | I have already arrived
exercise: Which statement about patois is true? | It is only spoken in schools | It has no grammar | *It is a full way of speaking, with its own rules | It is bad English that people should avoid
test: Jamaican Patois grew from which two things? | *English mixed with West African languages | English and Spanish only | Latin and Greek | French and Chinese
test: Which example shows patois as the special talk of a group? | A shop sign in London | *The jargon a team uses that only its members understand | A list of school rules | A map of Birmingham
exam: Why might a writer let a character speak patois in a story? | To make the story shorter | To hide the meaning from the reader | *To show where the character comes from | To prove English is the only true language
